  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nThe warming trend continues, with a warmer and more humid\nearly morning in tow for all but northeast portions of the \nforecast area, in response to mid/upper-level high pressure \nnudging in from the southwest.\n\nThe high remains anchored over the Tennessee Valley, southern\nAppalachians and Carolinas through Thursday, maintaining the\nheat, but leaving some room on its northeast periphery for short\nwave troughs/convective complexes to possibly graze northern /\nnortheastern portions of the forecast area. There, modest but \nadequate deep layer bulk shear, 30 to 35 kts at times associated\nwith the various short waves, plentiful moisture with PW values\nof 1.5 to 1.75 inches, and afternoon CAPE of 2 to 3 KJ/kg, \ncould lead to storms becoming strong to even marginally severe.\n\nTiming of periodic episodes of convection impacting northern / \nnortheastern portions of the forecast area is this and Thursday \nafternoon, with an overnight/early Wednesday morning feature not\nhaving the favor of diurnal timing. The Day 1 and Day 2 Severe \nWeather Outlooks continue to graze far northern / northeastern \nportions of the forecast area with a Marginal Risk for Severe \nThunderstorms, with Slight Risks northeast of that, outside the\nforecast area. The main hazards associated with the Marginal \nRisks are damaging wind gusts and large hail.\n\nElsewhere will be mainly dry and, for much of the lowlands, hot\nenough to necessitate the issuance of Heat Advisories. H85 \ntemperatures peak around 20-21C the next three afternoons, which\nsupports lowland high temperatures in the mid to upper 90s F. \nWith dew points in the low 70s F, heat indices are likely to \nreach the low 100s this afternoon, and the low to mid 100s \nWednesday and Thursday afternoons, the highest values centered \nacross the Huntington Tri-state and Charleston Metro areas. The \nHeat Advisories Wednesday may eventually need extended in area \nand / or time (for Thursday afternoon) and possibly even \nupgraded if confidence increases in Heat Index values reaching \nand exceeding 105 F in more than brief, isolated instances, \nparticularly on Wednesday and Thursday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nThe ridge amplifies but retrogrades westward toward the end of \nthe work week, allowing s short wave trough to drive a surface\ncold front toward the area from the northwest Thursday night \ninto Friday, and then through the area Friday afternoon and\nnight, and possibly even beyond. This increases the chance for \n/ coverage of showers and storms and, eventually, a break in the\noppressive heat. The threat for strong, heavy thunderstorms is \npossible amid adequate moisture and instability, and at least \nmarginal/modest shear. Current timing suggests the greatest \nthreat is still northern / northeastern portions of the area \nlate Thursday afternoon and evening, and then the bulk of the \nforecast area Friday afternoon and evening.\n\nThe Storm Prediction Center has placed a Slight Risk for Severe\nThunderstorms for northern into central portions of the \nforecast area for Thursday afternoon and evening, with a \nMarginal Risk for central into southern portions of the area. A\n15 percent Severe Thunderstorm Risk remains in place for all \nbut far southern portions of the forecast area for Friday/Friday\nnight, with a Marginal Risk for Excessive Rainfall out of the \nWeather Prediction Center.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...\n\nModel solutions have diverged on the timing of the cold front \ncrossing the area, and hence of the drier and somewhat cooler \nair arriving behind it this weekend. This blurs the forecast at \nleast for Saturday, when there is now a good chance for mainly \nafternoon showers and thunderstorms. Severe Weather Saturday \ndepends upon whether the front holds up coincident with another\nshort wave trough riding along the mid-upper-level northwest \nflow crossing the area in the afternoon.\n\nSurface high pressure pressure eventually builds south from \neastern Canada behind the cold front, with drier weather and \nmore seasonable temperatures bringing a break in the oppressive \nheat sometime over the Labor Day Holiday weekend. It make even take\nuntil after Sunday to completely shake the chance for showers \nand thunderstorms out of the forecast area, as we remain on the \nnortheast periphery of the mid/upper-level high large and in \ncharge over the center of the CONUS.",
